  "key_features": {
    "classic_presentation": "ONSET: GRADUAL, CHRONIC/RECURRENT over weeks to months (not acute). Often after meals or when lying down. LOCATION: RETROSTERNAL (behind breastbone) and EPIGASTRIC. BURNING rises from stomach toward throat. No radiation to arms/jaw. DURATION: Episodes last minutes to hours. Can be constant if severe reflux. May persist after meals. CHARACTER: BURNING sensation (heartburn). NOT sharp or pressure-like (would suggest cardiac). Acid rising sensation. AGGRAVATING: WORSE AFTER MEALS, when LYING DOWN, or BENDING OVER. Spicy foods, caffeine, alcohol, large meals trigger. RELIEVING: ANTACIDS provide quick relief. SITTING UPRIGHT helps. Elevation of head during sleep helps. TIMING: EPISODIC - worse after meals, at night when lying flat. Pattern repeats with dietary triggers. SEVERITY: 3-6/10, mild to moderate discomfort. Not severe. ASSOCIATED SYMPTOMS: SOUR TASTE (acid regurgitation), belching, dysphagia (if chronic), chronic cough, hoarseness. KEY POSITIVES: Antacid relief, worse when lying down, chronic pattern, dietary triggers. KEY NEGATIVES: NEW SEVERE chest pain requires cardiac workup FIRST. Dysphagia with weight loss suggests stricture/cancer. Hematemesis suggests ulcer.",
